[FINAL-TEXT] I have all the evidence needed. The picture is clear and definitive.

**Summary of findings:**

| Port | Device | Bridge | Working? |
|------|--------|--------|----------|
| switch1 eth2 | pc5 | `br_vlan1` ← **WRONG** | No — isolated |
| switch1 eth3 | pc6 | `br_vlan10` ← correct | Yes |
| switch1 eth4 | pc7 | `br_vlan10` ← correct | Yes |
| switch1 eth5 | pc8 | `br_vlan10` ← correct | Yes |

The uplink carries VLAN 10 via `eth0.10` / `eth1.10`, both correctly in `br_vlan10`. PC5's port (`eth2`) is the only one misconfigured — it landed in `br_vlan1`. Switch4 is not involved in PC5's path at all.

---

```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch1
diagnosis_text:
The reported fault is real but the ticket's localisation is wrong. Switch4 is
not involved in PC5's path — PC5 (172.16.10.5/24) connects to switch1 eth2,
which trunks up to mswitch0 and onward, not to switch4. Switch4 carries only
VLANs 5 and 6.

The actual fault is on switch1: port eth2 (the access port for pc5) has been
assigned to bridge br_vlan1 instead of br_vlan10. Every other VLAN-10 access
port on the same switch — eth3 (pc6), eth4 (pc7), eth5 (pc8) — is correctly
enslaved to br_vlan10, and those hosts communicate fine with each other and the
gateway (172.16.10.254). The uplink subinterfaces eth0.10 and eth1.10 are also
correctly in br_vlan10, so tagged VLAN 10 frames traverse the trunk without
issue. Only eth2 is in the wrong bridge, placing pc5 in an isolated L2 domain
(br_vlan1) that carries no VLAN-10 traffic and has no path to the default
gateway or any peer. This is confirmed by "Destination Host Unreachable" from
pc5 toward pc6, and successful pc6↔pc7/pc8 pings.

Root cause: switch1's startup configuration assigns eth2 (pc5's access port) to
br_vlan1 instead of br_vlan10, silently isolating pc5 from the VLAN-10 segment.

Corrective fix: on switch1, move eth2 to the correct bridge:
  ip link set eth2 nomaster
  ip link set eth2 master br_vlan10
and persist the change in the startup configuration.
=== END ===
```